# What no command here can answer, and the panel that answers it.
#
# `judges  source  text`, comma separated, and each judge is one word. `.foundry/gates` holds the
# half a command can settle. This holds the half it cannot.
#
# **A member is a model at a bench**, written `<model>:<role>`. The role is the part played, and
# Panel ships the parts. The model is who stands there.
#
# **Every member must approve, and one dissent stops the work.** A majority would let the members who
# looked hardest be outvoted by the ones who did not.
#
# A repository may name whoever it trusts. `panel:adversary` on one model is a real check: a
# different role reads for different faults, and finds them. What it cannot claim is a second
# mind, because it runs in the producing session on the same model.
#
# Foundry takes the stricter bar for itself, because what it judges here is its own runtime. That is
# this repository's choice and not a rule it ships.
#
# One member today, so this is a panel of one. A second model is the next piece of work.

codex:adversary  a change to what a run may claim is judged by something that did not write it
